    <gsmin:mindaturi rdf:resource="https://www.mindat.org/1:1:2304:3"/>
    <gsmin:mindaturl>http://www.mindat.org/min-2304.html</gsmin:mindaturl>
    <gsmin:rruffchemistry rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML">Ti&lt;sup&gt;4+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;V&lt;sup&gt;3+&lt;/sup&gt;O&lt;sub&gt;5&lt;/sub&gt;(OH)</gsmin:rruffchemistry>
    <gsmin:rruffnamehtml rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML">Kyzylkumite</gsmin:rruffnamehtml>
    <gsmin:rruffnameplain xml:lang="en">Kyzylkumite</gsmin:rruffnameplain>
    <gsmin:statusnotes xml:lang="en">Smyslova I G, Komkov A I, Pavshukov V V, Kuznetsova N V (1981) Kyzylkumite, V_2_Ti_3_O_9_, a new mineral of vanadium and titanium of a group of complex oxides, Zapiski Vsesoyuznogo Mineralogicheskogo Obshchestva 110(5), 607-612 Redefined: Raade G, Balic-Žunic T (2006) The crystal structure of (Be,[box])(V,Ti)_3_O_6_, a mineral related to kyzylkumite, The Canadian Mineralogist 44, 1147-1158 Chemical formula revised: Armbruster T, Lazic B, Reznitsky L Z, Sklyarov E V (2013) Kyzylkumite, Ti_2_V^3+^O_5_(OH): new structure type, modularity and revised formula, Mineralogical Magazine 77, 33-44</gsmin:statusnotes>
    <gsmin:strunzcodeV10>04.CB.75</gsmin:strunzcodeV10>
    <gsmin:strunzlabel xml:lang="en">Oxide, Metal: Oxygen = 2: 3,3: 5, and similar, With medium-sized cations</gsmin:strunzlabel>
    <gsmin:webmineralurl>http://www.webmineral.com/data/Kyzylkumite.shtml</gsmin:webmineralurl>
    <gsmin:wikipediadate>1980-081</gsmin:wikipediadate>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/mineral/minyulite">
