    <gsmin:rruffchemistry rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML">Pb&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;Fe&lt;sup&gt;3+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;(P&lt;sup&gt;5+&lt;/sup&gt;O&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;)&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;(OH)&lt;sub&gt;5&lt;/sub&gt;·H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O</gsmin:rruffchemistry>
    <gsmin:rruffnamehtml rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#HTML">Pattersonite</gsmin:rruffnamehtml>
    <gsmin:rruffnameplain xml:lang="en">Pattersonite</gsmin:rruffnameplain>
    <gsmin:statusnotes xml:lang="en">Kolitsch U, Bernhardt H-J, Krause W, Blass G (2008) Pattersonite, PbFe3(PO4)2(OH)4[(H2O)0.5(OH)0.5]2, a new supergene phosphate mineral: description and crystal structure, European Journal of Mineralogy 20, 281-288 Poirier G, Ercit T S, Tait K T, Piilonen P C, Rowe R (2009) New mineral names, American Mineralogist 94, 399-408</gsmin:statusnotes>
    <gsmin:strunzcodeV10>08.BL.25</gsmin:strunzcodeV10>
    <gsmin:strunzlabel xml:lang="en">Phosphate, etc. with additional anions, without H2O, With medium-sized and large cations, (OH, etc.):RO4 = 3:1</gsmin:strunzlabel>
    <gsmin:webmineralurl>http://www.webmineral.com/data/Pattersonite.shtml</gsmin:webmineralurl>
    <gsmin:wikipediadate>2005-049</gsmin:wikipediadate>
