    <gsmin:wikipediadate>1991-025</gsmin:wikipediadate>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/common/Solid">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Matter with a definite shape, mass, and volume, that does not conform to the shape of a container in which it is placed. Solids have a high density, meaning that the particles are tightly packed together. (https://www.livescience.com/46506-states-of-matter.html) A solid is characterized by structural rigidity and resistance to a force applied to the surface. The atoms in a solid are bound to each other, either in a regular geometric lattice (crystalline solids, which include metals and ordinary ice), or irregularly (an amorphous solid such as common window glass). Solids cannot be compressed with little pressure whereas gases can be compressed with little pressure because the molecules in a gas are loosely packed. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solid)</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Solid state of matter</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/common/State_Of_Matter_Value"/>
  </rdf:Description>
