  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/mineral/claymineral">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Informal term for a group of hydrous phyllosilicates: smectite, illite, chlorite and kandite, also  kaolinite, halloysite, montmorillonite or smectite. Clays are usually distinguished by geologists or pedologists from other fine-grained soils and sediments by their grain size and/or mineralogy, though geotechnical engineers distinguish them by plasticity. Silt has larger particle size than clays; geologists usually consider the division to occur at a particle size of 2 µm, sedimentologists often use 4–5 μm, and colloid chemists use 1 μm. Geotechnical engineers distinguish between silts and clays based on the plasticity properties of the soil, as measured by the soils' Atterberg limits. ISO 14688 grades clay particles as being smaller than 2 μm and silt particles as being larger. (based on https://www.mindat.org/min-49430.html)</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Clay Mineral</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:seeAlso xml:lang="en">https://www.mindat.org/min-1062.html</rdfs:seeAlso>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/geology/Mineral_Material"/>
    <gsmin:mindatid>min-49430</gsmin:mindatid>
  </rdf:Description>
